From 7b798a7c1cc7cf8c3cc268bfb739a7fbab5b423b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BOAR R&D <noreply>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:40:46 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] routing: answer emergency drinking-water questions with a
 fixed water-treatment card

"After a flood the tap water might be contaminated. How do I make water safe to drink?" got a travel guide's line ("Tap water is not safe to drink...") instead of how to treat the water. An English question about making drinking water safe in an emergency (flood, storm, outage, boil-water notice, contamination, a flooded well, only creek or lake water left) now gets a fixed card before any model, in the carbon-monoxide card's pattern: use bottled or stored water; follow the local notice; settle or filter cloudy water, then boil 1 minute (3 above 1,000 m); unscented bleach at 2 drops per litre for 5-9% bleach or 10 for 1% bleach, doubled for cloudy, coloured or very cold water, 30 minutes, chlorine-smell check; what boiling and bleach can't fix; flooded wells. The text is an independent reviewer's sentence-by-sentence match to CDC "How to Make Water Safe in an Emergency" and US EPA "Emergency Disinfection of Drinking Water" (public domain; both pages re-checked 2026-10-19, figures unchanged). AI-reviewed, not clinically reviewed. Receipt: answer:safety-fact-water, label "Safety facts (CDC, EPA)". Portuguese and Spanish questions are untouched.

The trigger needs a drinking-water subject, a make-safe ask, an emergency context and a present need, and stays off swimming, pools, laundry, cooking, appliances, plumbing, driving, history, how-it-works and backcountry filtering. It favours precision: on its last fresh set it fired on 18 of 25 water questions and on none of 75 others; a missed phrasing keeps the normal health route, so a miss costs nothing that exists today. On 640 public bench and self-test messages it fires twice, both water-treatment questions (dng-005 and the emergency pack's flood tap-water self-test). Three retrieval tests that used dng-005 now use a phrasing without a make-safe word, which still takes the health route.

